@display("Intent")
intent: section {
  | The intent [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) says why a product exists and for whom: its goals, the actors that cross
  | its boundary, what it deliberately leaves out, what it relies on others to do, and the
  | qualities that matter most. Intent is validated with the people it's for and measured in the
  | product's use; no test proves it. A whole product's intent, its vision, actors, personas and
  | top goals, opens the package; an area opens with the goals of its own.
  |
  | | Kind                   | Holds                                                                  |
  | | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | | `goal(refs…, measure)` | what the product is for, with an optional measure of success; its references name the actors or personas who asked for it |
  | | `actor(kind)`          | a role that crosses the boundary: `PERSON`, the default, `SYSTEM` or `AGENT` |
  | | `persona(plays)`       | an archetype of a person who plays an actor, with `goals` and `pains`   |
  | | `nongoal`              | something the product could plausibly do and deliberately doesn't       |
  | | `assumption`           | what the spec relies on an actor or the environment to do               |
  | | `quality(measure)`     | a quality that matters most, measured by its quality scenarios          |

  goal_kind: term {{
    `name: goal(refs…, measure = "…") {{ … }}`, an outcome the product is for, in the words of
    the people it's for, with no solution in it. A reference to an actor or a persona names who
    asked for it. Rules, use cases and constraints reference the goals they serve.
  }}

  actor_kind: term {{
    `name: actor(kind = ActorKind.SYSTEM) {{ … }}`, a role that crosses the system's
    boundary: a person, another program or an agent. It's the one "who" of a spec: rules bind
    actors and parts, use cases start from an actor, and personas play one.
  }}

  persona_kind: term {{
    `name: persona(plays = actor) {{ … }}`, an archetype of a person behind an actor, with
    what they want and what gets in their way. It never adds a second list of who.
  }}

  nongoal_kind: term {{
    `name: nongoal {{ … }}`, something a reader could expect the product to do that it
    deliberately doesn't, written as what's left out rather than as a behaviour.
  }}

  assumption_kind: term {{
    `name: assumption {{ … }}`, what the spec relies on an actor or the environment to do,
    such as an operator keeping a clock in sync. A rule, a constraint or a use case that depends on it
    references it.
  }}

  quality_kind: term {{
    `name: quality(measure = "…") {{ … }}`, one of the few qualities that matter most, as
    responsiveness or recovery. Its measurable form is a quality scenario that references it.
  }}

  intent_layer: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ give `goal`, `actor`, `persona`, `nongoal`, `assumption` and `quality` the `INTENT` [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer) }}
  }

  persona_plays_actor: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ a persona's `plays` is missing or names a node that isn't an actor }}
    must {{ report it as an error at the persona or at the reference }}
  }

  actor_kinds: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ read an actor's `kind` as `ActorKind.PERSON` when it's left out }}
  }

  references_named_kind: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ a reference that must name an actor, a goal or another kind names a node of a different kind }}
    must {{ report it as an error at the reference }}
  }

  goals_never_proven: rule(system.checker) {
    mustNot {{ require a proof or a status for a goal, a `nongoal`, an assumption, an actor, a persona or a quality }}
    then {{ conformance lists none of them }}
  }

  goal_measure_shown: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show a goal's measure and the actors or personas who asked for it under its text, and below them the [derived lists](#heron.language.specifications.layers.derived_list) of the rules, use cases, constraints and scenarios that serve it }}
  }

  actor_view: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show an actor with its kind, the personas that play it, and the use cases and rules that name it }}
  }

  stakeholders_as_source: rule(actors.author) {
    must {{ name who asked for a goal or a constraint by referencing its actor or persona, or, where no actor or persona is declared for them, in its prose, and declare no node only to name them }}
  }

  product_intent_on_top: rule(actors.author) {
    must {{ put a product's vision, actors, personas and top goals at the top of its package, and open each area with its own goals }}
  }

  @display("A product's intent")
  intent_example: example {
    | ```heron
    | import = ["std/doc@1"]
    |
    | self_service: goal(measure = "Nine refunds in ten need no support ticket.") {{
    |   Customers fix a wrong charge themselves, as the support team asked.
    | }}
    |
    | customer: actor {{ Someone who paid with the shop. }}
    |
    | bank: actor(kind = ActorKind.SYSTEM) {{ The card network that settles payments. }}
    |
    | priya: persona(plays = customer) {{ Buys weekly, on her phone, between meetings. }}
    |
    | no_cash_refunds: nongoal {{ Refunds in cash at a store. }}
    |
    | bank_settles: assumption {{ The [bank] settles a refund within five working days. }}
    |
    | responsive: quality(measure = "Pages answer within a second.") {{ The shop feels instant. }}
    | ```
  }

  errors: section {
    @display("Not an actor")
    not_an_actor: error("type") {
      title = "{name} is a {kind}, not an actor"
      name: field(String)
      kind: field(String)
      | A reference that must name an actor naming another kind of node; any `Ref(kind)` is checked the same way.
      | Reported as [persona_plays_actor] and [references_named_kind] say.
    }
  }
}
